WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.080
Hey, nobody's perfect.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.232
Avengers: Endgame might be the biggest movie
in the world, but that doesn't mean that it's

00:00:06.232 --> 00:00:10.470
immune from those nagging continuity errors
that can be found in virtually every Hollywood

00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:15.390
production, including many of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe entries that came before it.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:20.490
In case you're not hip to the lingo, a "continuity
error," such as the ones that plagued 2008's

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:25.620
Iron Man, usually happens when a cut between
scenes imply a series of events that, well,

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:30.119
shouldn't be possible, such as a character's
drink suddenly going from empty to full from

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.800
one shot to the next.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.980
In Endgame, fans have spotted a pretty egregious
example, and it has to do with Scott Lang.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:41.010
You may only want to keep watching if you've
seen the flick, because we'll be getting into

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.190
some SPOILERS.

00:00:43.190 --> 00:00:47.170
The glitch occurs near the end of the film,
during the monumental battle between Thanos

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.559
and his invading forces and the Avengers,
alongside all of the returned heroes and the

00:00:51.559 --> 00:00:53.319
entire Wakandan army.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.880
But you don't need a whole recap, right?

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:56.320
"Avengers!

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.840
So they're fighting this purple guy over the
universe, and then he won, and now you're

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:01.840
here…

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.280
Anyway."

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.960
During the melee, Lang and Hope Van Dyne are
making their way through the chaos in an attempt

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.240
to reach Scott's van, still housing the Quantum
Tunnel.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.840
It's a nifty little scene, with Ant-Man and
the Wasp suddenly thrust back into action

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.439
together once again… but the very next shot
is a wide one of the two opposing forces clashing

00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:22.500
on the battlefield.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:27.680
There, in the middle of it all, is Lang in
his Giant-Man form.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:28.830
Whoops!

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:32.689
Directors Joe and Anthony Russo have been
going gangbusters explaining to the press

00:01:32.689 --> 00:01:36.500
some of Endgame's perceived plot holes and
mistakes, but unless they're going to tell

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.840
us that it was actually Hank Pym or Bill Foster
wearing an extra Ant-Man suit or something,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:46.070
we're pretty sure this is a big old fat error
that falls directly on the film's editors.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:50.999
Film productions generally employ one or more
"script supervisors" whose job it is to make

00:01:50.999 --> 00:01:55.189
sure that seemingly insignificant details
remain consistent between multiple takes of

00:01:55.189 --> 00:01:56.340
the same scene.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:00.000
When they drop the ball, things can start
getting a little glitchy, but often, such

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:04.579
as in the case of this scene, it comes down
to a simple oversight during the editing stage.

00:02:04.579 --> 00:02:08.810
If the sequence had been cut differently,
it may have appeared that Lang had left Van

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:13.750
Dyne on her own in order to "go big" and rejoin
the fray, but as it stands, he appears to

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:15.830
be in two places at the same time.

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.780
Ant-Man may be resourceful, but he's no Loki.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.330
As we previously mentioned, it can be nearly
impossible to squeeze every last continuity

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:27.920
error out of a film, editors are often left
with a less-than-desirable choice of shots

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:31.990
to piece together, and continuity can be tough
to maintain when various takes of the same

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.300
scene are shot weeks apart, for example.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.080
The Mighty Marvel Cinematic Universe is no
exception, and trained as its rabid fans are

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:43.890
to always be on the lookout for tiny, Easter
egg-y details, very few of its continuity

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.140
errors go unnoticed.

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:50.360
The "Goofs" section of the IMDb page for inaugural
entry Iron Man alone lists a whopping two

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:55.390
dozen mistakes in continuity, including Stark's
tip disappearing from the tip jar, the color

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.730
of the camera lens on Tony's phone changing
between cuts, and even the length of Stark's

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.900
goatee going from pencil-thin to full and
fuzzy over the course of one short night.

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.040
Mistakes such as these are pretty easy to
spot if you're on the lookout for them, and

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:12.320
let's face it, the only real surprise here
is that this is the only continuity error

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.650
that has been spotted in Endgame so far.

00:03:14.650 --> 00:03:18.700
With the flick only having been in theaters
for a short time, it probably won't be long

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.250
before fans going back for repeat viewings
point out a bunch more.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.680
Does it seem a little bit ridiculous for such
an easily-spotted gaffe to show up in such

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.060
a highly-anticipated box office juggernaut
of a film?

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.760
A little bit, sure.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.200
Does said gaffe diminish our enjoyment of
Avengers: Endgame even one iota?

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:37.240
Absolutely not.

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:41.870
Then again, it's a little tougher than usual
to zero in on tiny onscreen errors when you're

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.150
crying tears of pure joy.

